<!-- 2. DÉMARCHE -->
<section class="section" id="demarche">
  <div class="grille">
    <div class="grille-a">
      <h2 class="titre-2">Your body, your&nbsp;choices</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="grille-b">
      <p>My approach is feminist because it starts from one principle: every person has the right to clear, complete information in line with current scientific knowledge, to free, informed and revocable consent, and to care that respects their privacy, their history and their identity.</p>
      <p>I remain attentive to medical, obstetric and gynaecological violence, and to personal histories marked by sexual violence. I adapt my care to what you are ready to experience.</p>
      <p>I also support trans and non-binary people through their gender affirmation journey, with or without surgery: pre-operative preparation and post-operative recovery, pelvic floor rehabilitation, pain and comfort. You are welcomed with your name and your pronouns, with no intrusive curiosity and nothing to justify.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 3. DOMAINES DE PRISE EN CHARGE -->
<section class="section" id="domaines">
  <div class="intro">
    <h2>Areas of care</h2>
    <p>Four areas, one way of working: listening, explaining, then suggesting exercises suited to your daily life.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="domaines">
    <article class="domaine">
      <h3>Female and male pelvic floor</h3>
      <div class="domaine-texte">
        <p>Urinary leakage, pelvic pain or pain during sex, pelvic organ prolapse, recovery after childbirth, prostate surgery or medical treatment: the pelvic floor concerns every body, at every age. I also provide anorectal rehabilitation (anal incontinence, constipation) and pessary fitting. You are equally welcome to come simply to better understand this part of the body, which is still talked about far too little.</p>
        <p class="reperes">Urinary leakage · Pelvic pain · Prolapse · Postpartum · Prostatectomy · Surgery</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="domaine">
      <h3>Pregnancy and postpartum</h3>
      <div class="domaine-texte">
        <p>During pregnancy, physiotherapy relieves back, pelvic and pubic pain and prepares the body for birth. Staying active is also part of care: I help you continue or adapt your physical activity, safely, in every trimester. After the birth, we rebuild step by step: pelvic floor, deep abdominal muscles, then a gradual return to activity.</p>
        <p class="reperes">Low back and pelvic pain · Adapted physical activity · Pelvic floor preparation · Abdominal rehabilitation</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="domaine">
      <h3>Infants</h3>
      <div class="domaine-texte">
        <p>Torticollis, a flattening head, a marked preference for one side, foot malposition, delayed motor milestones: I work with little ones gently, through play and movement. Parents are always involved: your observations and your intuition are invaluable, and I show you the movements to repeat at home.</p>
        <p class="reperes">Torticollis · Plagiocephaly · Foot malpositions · Motor development</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="domaine">
      <h3>Scars</h3>
      <div class="domaine-texte">
        <p>Caesarean section, episiotomy, perineal tear, abdominal or pelvic surgery, top surgery: a scar can remain sensitive, pull or restrict movement, sometimes long afterwards. Manual therapy, cupping and TECAR therapy soften the scar and soothe the area, and I show you self-massage techniques to continue at home.</p>
        <p class="reperes">Caesarean section · Episiotomy and tears · Gender-affirming surgery</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

