<!-- 7. QUESTIONS FRÉQUENTES -->
<section class="section" id="questions">
  <div class="grille">
    <div class="grille-a">
      <h2 class="titre-2">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <p>There are no embarrassing questions. If yours is not here, write to me.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq grille-b">
      <details>
        <summary>When should pelvic floor rehabilitation start after a birth?</summary>
        <p>Generally six to eight weeks after delivery, once the postnatal check-up has taken place. If you are in pain or your situation requires it, I can see you earlier, with your doctor's agreement. It is never too late: rehabilitation remains useful months, even years, later.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is pelvic floor rehabilitation painful?</summary>
        <p>No. The techniques are gentle and gradual, and nothing is done without your consent. If something is uncomfortable, we adapt it or do it differently.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which pelvic floor rehabilitation techniques do you use?</summary>
        <p>Biofeedback with a probe, electrical stimulation, ultrasound biofeedback or manual rehabilitation: the choice depends on the assessment, your symptoms and what you prefer.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I come with my baby?</summary>
        <p>Yes, of course. The practice is accessible with a pushchair, and you can feed or change your child during the session if needed.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How many sessions should I expect?</summary>
        <p>It depends on your situation. Pelvic floor rehabilitation often takes around ten sessions; for an infant, a few sessions are sometimes enough. We review progress regularly.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What if the fee above the reimbursed rate is an obstacle for me?</summary>
        <p>If you are in financial difficulty, write to me before booking an appointment. Cost should not make you give up on care.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

